//// Front 12 step 4.4 — "no external target for the active backend". A
//// host-backed `declare fn` bound to one host is a program for that host: on
//// erlang (and beam, which consumes the Erlang vocabulary) it runs and prints
//// `hi`; on commonJS the compiler **refuses** it, naming the declaration and the
//// backend — `external_erlang_only.commonJS.expect` carries that diagnostic, the
//// shape of a `reject/` cell on one target.
////
//// wasm refuses it too — `external_erlang_only.wasm.expect`, the same sentence
//// with its own backend named. It did not until `fix/wasm-refusals`: measured
//// at `85f883bd` and again at `fa30a214`, `wat.zig` (`lowerPlainCall`) lowered
//// a host-backed `declare fn` with no wasm host to `unreachable` "so the module
//// still loads", and the program trapped at run time (exit 134, stdout empty)
//// where the other three answered at compile time. This cell held an
//// `external_erlang_only.targets` sidecar keeping wasm out for that reason;
//// decision 67's refusal landed, so the sidecar is gone and all four run here.

#[@External.Erlang("erlang", "list_to_binary")]
declare fn listToBinary(codes: i32[]) -> string;

pub fn main() {
    @print(listToBinary([104, 105]));
}
